\emph{Structural confound: token length.}
In continuation mode the generation prompt is discarded, so all depths $\geq 1$ consist entirely of machine-generated tokens and the 1v2 boundary compares texts differing only in conditioning context (human-authored vs.\ machine-generated prompt), not structural composition.
Depth~0 texts average ${\sim}$191 subword tokens (C4 truncation), while depth~1+ texts average 193--256 tokens depending on model (Table~\ref{tab:token_length}).
This asymmetry is directionally opposite to a length-inflation confound: shorter depth-0 texts provide less feature-extraction context, making 0v1 discrimination harder, not easier.
Within depths 1--5, mean token counts are stable (within-model range $\leq$ 4 tokens for all configurations except Qwen-continuation at 6 tokens), so the pairwise comparisons underlying $\Kempirical{=}2$ are unaffected by length.
EOS truncation rates are similarly depth-stable within each model: Qwen-rewrite 7--11\%, Pythia 34--36\%, OLMo $<$1\%, Qwen-continuation 17--20\%, Qwen-7B 45--47\%.
Models with the highest truncation (Pythia, Qwen-7B) still yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ with the same step-function AUC decay pattern as low-truncation models (Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci}), confirming that EOS truncation does not drive the boundary.

\emph{Graduated filtering.}
All $\alpha$ values (0.1--0.9) underperform binary-ours on MMLU ($\alpha{=}0.1$: 0.5873; $\alpha{=}0.9$: 0.5892 vs.\ 0.5941); at $\alpha{=}0.9$, HellaSwag matches exactly (0.6701).
Binary-ours significantly outperforms all alternatives on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} \leq 0.021$); Graduated$_{0.9}$ is indistinguishable on HellaSwag ($p = 1.00$) but worse on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$).
\label{tab:pairwise_significance}

\subsection{Dose-Response: Contamination Ratio Effect}
\label{sec:dose_response}

The main filtering experiments (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}) use 100\% synthetic data to maximize effect size.
To characterize behavior at lower contamination ratios, we mix synthetic depths 1--5 with clean C4 at 10\%, 30\%, and 50\% contamination (Qwen-1.5B continuation, 5 seeds each; Table~\ref{tab:dose_response}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{MMLU and HellaSwag by contamination ratio and filtering strategy (5 seeds, mean $\pm$ std). Base model MMLU: 0.6018. Binary filtering retains a significant advantage on HellaSwag across all ratios ($p \leq 0.004$, Holm-Bonferroni corrected).}
\label{tab:dose_response}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{10\%}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{30\%}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{50\%}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\cmidrule(lr){6-7}
\textbf{Strategy} & MMLU & HellaSwag & MMLU & HellaSwag & MMLU & HellaSwag \\
\midrule
No-filter   & $.6058{\pm}.0002$ & $.6689{\pm}.0006$ & $.6069{\pm}.0007$ & $.6664{\pm}.0018$ & $.6063{\pm}.0012$ & $.6680{\pm}.0005$ \\
Binary-ours & $\mathbf{.6079{\pm}.0004}$ & $\mathbf{.6727{\pm}.0007}$ & $\mathbf{.6082{\pm}.0011}$ & $\mathbf{.6725{\pm}.0006}$ & $\mathbf{.6088{\pm}.0009}$ & $\mathbf{.6731{\pm}.0004}$ \\
Graduated   & $.6066{\pm}.0010$ & $.6700{\pm}.0014$ & $.6072{\pm}.0008$ & $.6665{\pm}.0012$ & $.6062{\pm}.0008$ & $.6679{\pm}.0001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

At all tested ratios (10--50\%), no-filter MMLU remains above the base model (0.6018), indicating that synthetic data at these proportions is net-beneficial rather than harmful.
Binary filtering consistently improves over no-filter, with HellaSwag differences significant across all ratios ($p \leq 0.004$, Holm-Bonferroni corrected).
MMLU differences are smaller and significant only at 10\% after correction ($p = 0.001$).
Graduated weighting is indistinguishable from no-filter at all ratios (MMLU $p \geq 0.27$; HellaSwag $p \geq 0.06$), confirming that depth assignments beyond $\Kempirical$ inject noise regardless of contamination severity.
Effect sizes diminish 3--6$\times$ relative to the 100\% setting (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}), but the strategy ordering (binary $>$ graduated $\approx$ no-filter) is preserved.

\subsection{Feature Subset Stability}
\label{sec:feature_subset}

To test whether $\Kempirical{=}2$ depends on the specific 15D feature combination, we evaluate random feature subsets of varying size on Pythia-1.4B prompt-chain data (Table~\ref{tab:feature_subset}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{1v2 $\auc$ by feature subset size (Pythia-1.4B). $\Kempirical{=}2$ ($\auc > \theta{=}0.60$) is robust at $\geq$8 features.}
\label{tab:feature_subset}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subset} & \textbf{Tested} & \textbf{Pass} & \textbf{Min $\auc$} & \textbf{Mean $\auc$} \\
\midrule
15D (full) & 1 & 1 & 0.6262 & 0.6262 \\
10D        & 5 & 5 & 0.6153 & 0.6191 \\
8D         & 5 & 5 & 0.6056 & 0.6159 \\
5D         & 5 & 2 & 0.5856 & 0.5998 \\
3D (top-3) & 1 & 0 & 0.5871 & 0.5871 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

$\Kempirical{=}2$ holds for all subsets of size $\geq$8 (10/10 pass, min $\auc = 0.6056$), becomes fragile at 5D (2/5 pass), and fails at 3D (top-3 surprisal features alone: $\auc = 0.5871 < \theta$).
The boundary does not depend on any specific feature combination but requires sufficient dimensionality ($\geq$8) to capture the multi-faceted distributional shift between generations 1 and 2.

\subsection{self\_BLEU Feature Independence}
\label{sec:selfbleu_ablation}

To verify that $\Kempirical$ does not depend on the self\_BLEU feature (which may reflect stylistic repetition rather than depth-correlated degradation), we compare 15D and 14D (self\_BLEU removed) classifiers across four model configurations (Table~\ref{tab:selfbleu_ablation}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{15D vs.\ 14D (self\_BLEU removed) 1v2 $\auc$ with bootstrap 95\% CIs. Removing self\_BLEU does not change $\Kempirical$ for any configuration. Maximum $|\Delta\auc| = 0.0031$, within bootstrap CI width (${\sim}0.018$).}
\label{tab:selfbleu_ablation}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & $\Kempirical$ & \textbf{15D $\auc$ [CI]} & \textbf{14D $\auc$ [CI]} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-base   & 2/2 & $.6288$ $[.620,\,.638]$ & $.6258$ $[.616,\,.635]$ & $-.0031$ \\
Pythia-1.4B  & 2/2 & $.8840$ $[.878,\,.890]$ & $.8830$ $[.876,\,.889]$ & $-.0010$ \\
LLaMA-8B     & 2/2 & $.6075$ $[.598,\,.617]$ & $.6074$ $[.598,\,.617]$ & $-.0001$ \\
Mistral-7B   & 1/1 & $.5902$ $[.580,\,.600]$ & $.5903$ $[.580,\,.600]$ & $+.0002$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

All four configurations preserve their $\Kempirical$ under 14D, with negligible AUC change ($|\Delta| \leq 0.003$).
The self\_BLEU feature contributes minimally to the depth boundary; $\Kempirical{=}2$ is driven by surprisal and entropy features rather than lexical repetition statistics.

\subsection{Wikipedia Domain: Scorer Confound Resolution}
\label{sec:wiki_domain}

Under same-family scoring (Qwen-1.5B scorer), Wikipedia $\Kempirical$ appeared to exhibit model-family dependence: Qwen generators (1.5B and 7B) yielded $\Kempirical{=}2$, while all four non-Qwen models yielded $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Table~\ref{tab:wiki_domain}).
A scorer confound ablation resolves this apparent split.
Rescoring the same Qwen-7B Wikipedia text with Pythia-1.4B reduces 1v2 $\auc$ from $0.611$ to $0.587$ ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp), dropping $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1.
With the scorer confound controlled, all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ on Wikipedia, and the domain ordering simplifies to: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $>$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).

